From b0e42f02ea8308b7002f7be9784cb82e7b117b55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nganaremba-rem <ngancloverr+remkugithub@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:09:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 14/23] emoji insert: leave the glyph on the clipboard and
 paste with real key codes

Typing with wtype skipped the clipboard and did nothing in Chromium apps,
which ignore the private keymap wtype needs. The glyph is now copied with
every text format on offer and pasted through uclip's uinput backend
(ydotool), which also reaches X11 clients.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 bin/omarchy-menu-emoji-insert | 14 ++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bin/omarchy-menu-emoji-insert b/bin/omarchy-menu-emoji-insert
index 92cf4fd..c8e5d03 100755
--- a/bin/omarchy-menu-emoji-insert
+++ b/bin/omarchy-menu-emoji-insert
@@ -9,8 +9,14 @@ emoji="${1:-}"
 
 [[ -n $emoji ]] || exit
 
-# niri port: type the glyph itself. Upstream copies it and presses Shift+Insert,
-# which on this desktop pastes the primary selection (the last text selected
-# with the mouse) instead of the clipboard.
+# niri port: leave the glyph on the clipboard and paste it with the keys the
+# focused app expects (Ctrl+Shift+V in terminals, Ctrl+V elsewhere). Upstream
+# pressed Shift+Insert, which here pastes the primary selection (the last text
+# selected with the mouse). Typing the glyph with wtype skips the clipboard,
+# and X11 and Chromium apps ignore the private keymap wtype needs, so the
+# paste keys go out as real key codes (uclip's uinput backend).
+# No --type: wl-copy then offers every text format, including the UTF-8 ones
+# terminals and X11 apps ask for; "text/plain" alone made them paste nothing.
+printf '%s' "$emoji" | wl-copy
 sleep 0.15
-wtype -- "$emoji"
+UCLIP_BACKEND=uinput "$HOME/.config/niri/scripts/uclip" paste
